=== tt instance start --help
Start the instances the targets select. By default every instance runs in the
background under a watchdog of tt, which restarts it as the restart field of its
record says.

With --foreground tt stays in the foreground, for systemd, a container or a
terminal, while every instance runs under a watchdog of its own that does not
restart it. The output of the instances goes to their tt.log and to the standard
output, prefixed with the instance when there are several. SIGINT, SIGTERM and
SIGQUIT stop every instance, as a stop of its watchdog does; tt instance stop
stops one of them from another terminal. An instance that ends does not stop the
others. tt exits once all of them have ended: with 0 when they were stopped in
time, with the exit code of the first instance that failed otherwise, 137 for
one killed as it did not stop in time.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt instance start [<APP>:<INSTANCE> | <APP>:*][,...] [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
      --foreground                   stay in the foreground while the instances
                                     run, without restarting them
  -h, --help                         help for start
      --integrity-check-period int   how often the watchdog re-checks integrity,
                                     in seconds
      --scope string                 scope the application is looked for in:
                                     project, user or system
      --template string              template of the application the instances
                                     are described in (default "default")

=== tt cluster publish --help
Publish an application or an instance configuration to a cluster configuration
file, to a etcd URI or to a tarantool config storage URI.

An application gets the configuration in the config.yaml of its pair, or in the
config storage its config.yaml points to with config.etcd or config.storage. A
configuration that drops an instance of the cluster instances.yaml of the
application names is refused; the instances its "*" stands for may be dropped.

The URL specifies a etcd or tarantool config storage connection settings in the
following format:
http(s)://[username:password@]host:port/prefix[?arguments]

* prefix - a base path to Tarantool configuration in etcd or tarantool config
  storage.

Possible arguments:
* key - a target configuration key in the prefix.
* name - a name of an instance in the cluster configuration.
* timeout - a request timeout in seconds (default 3.0).
* ssl_key_file - a path to a private SSL key file.
* ssl_cert_file - a path to an SSL certificate file.
* ssl_ca_file - a path to a trusted certificate authorities (CA) file.
* ssl_ca_path - a path to a trusted certificate authorities (CA) directory.
* ssl_ciphers - a list of allowed SSL ciphers.
* verify_host - set off (default true) verification of the certificate’s name
  against the host.
* verify_peer - set off (default true) verification of the peer’s SSL
  certificate.

The command supports the following environment variables:
* TT_CLI_USERNAME - specifies a Tarantool username;
* TT_CLI_PASSWORD - specifies a Tarantool password.
* TT_CLI_ETCD_USERNAME - specifies a Etcd username;
* TT_CLI_ETCD_PASSWORD - specifies a Etcd password.

The priority of credentials:
environment variables < command flags < URL credentials.

HTTPS connection behavior:
* The https:// scheme enables TLS for both etcd and Tarantool Config Storage
  (TCS), even when no ssl_* URL arguments are specified.
* For etcd, certificate verification is skipped if neither ssl_ca_file nor
  ssl_ca_path is specified. A configured CA enables verification unless
  explicitly disabled.
* TCS keeps the certificate verification behavior of its TLS backend. The
  default Go backend uses system CAs unless ssl_ca_file is specified.
* Do not mix http:// and https:// addresses in an etcd endpoint list. If any
  endpoint uses https://, TLS is enabled for all endpoints.

By default, the command removes all keys in etcd with prefix '/prefix/config/'
and writes the result to '/prefix/config/all'. You could work and update a
target key with the 'key' argument.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt cluster publish (<APP> | <APP>:<INSTANCE> | <URI>) file [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
      --force                         force publish and skip validation
      --group string                  group name
  -h, --help                          help for publish
  -p, --password string               password (used as etcd credentials only)
      --replicaset string             replicaset name
      --scope string                  scope the application is looked for in:
                                      project, user or system
      --template string               template of the application the instances
                                      are described in (default "default")
  -u, --username string               username (used as etcd credentials only)
      --with-integrity-check string   path to the private key that signs
                                      published data and packages
[0;1;39m
EXAMPLES[0m
  tt cluster publish application_name cluster.yaml
  tt cluster publish application_name:instance_name instance.yaml
  tt cluster publish https://user:pass@localhost:2379/tt cluster.yaml
  tt cluster publish https://user:pass@localhost:2379/tt?name=instance
  instance.yaml
  tt cluster publish --group group --replicaset replicaset
  https://user:pass@localhost:2379/tt?name=instance instance.yaml

=== tt cluster replicaset promote --help
Promote an instance

The URL specifies a etcd or tarantool config storage connection settings in the
following format:
http(s)://[username:password@]host:port/prefix[?arguments]

* prefix - a base path to Tarantool configuration in etcd or tarantool config
  storage.

Possible arguments:
* key - a target configuration key in the prefix.
* name - a name of an instance in the cluster configuration.
* timeout - a request timeout in seconds (default 3.0).
* ssl_key_file - a path to a private SSL key file.
* ssl_cert_file - a path to an SSL certificate file.
* ssl_ca_file - a path to a trusted certificate authorities (CA) file.
* ssl_ca_path - a path to a trusted certificate authorities (CA) directory.
* ssl_ciphers - a list of allowed SSL ciphers.
* verify_host - set off (default true) verification of the certificate’s name
  against the host.
* verify_peer - set off (default true) verification of the peer’s SSL
  certificate.

The command supports the following environment variables:
* TT_CLI_USERNAME - specifies a Tarantool username;
* TT_CLI_PASSWORD - specifies a Tarantool password.
* TT_CLI_ETCD_USERNAME - specifies a Etcd username;
* TT_CLI_ETCD_PASSWORD - specifies a Etcd password.

The priority of credentials:
environment variables < command flags < URL credentials.

HTTPS connection behavior:
* The https:// scheme enables TLS for both etcd and Tarantool Config Storage
  (TCS), even when no ssl_* URL arguments are specified.
* For etcd, certificate verification is skipped if neither ssl_ca_file nor
  ssl_ca_path is specified. A configured CA enables verification unless
  explicitly disabled.
* TCS keeps the certificate verification behavior of its TLS backend. The
  default Go backend uses system CAs unless ssl_ca_file is specified.
* Do not mix http:// and https:// addresses in an etcd endpoint list. If any
  endpoint uses https://, TLS is enabled for all endpoints.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt cluster replicaset promote [-f] [flags] <URI> <INSTANCE_NAME>
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -f, --force                         skip selecting a key for patching
  -h, --help                          help for promote
  -p, --password string               password (used as etcd/tarantool config
                                      storage credentials)
  -u, --username string               username (used as etcd/tarantool config
                                      storage credentials)
      --with-integrity-check string   path to the private key that signs
                                      published data and packages

=== tt replicaset vshard bootstrap --help
Bootstrap vshard in the cluster.

The command supports the following Tarantool environment variables:
* TT_CLI_USERNAME - specifies a Tarantool username
* TT_CLI_PASSWORD - specifies a Tarantool password

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt replicaset vshard bootstrap [--config|--custom] [--timeout secs] [flags]
  (<APP> | <APP>:<INSTANCE> | <URI>)

  The URI can be specified in the following formats:
  * [tcp://][username:password@][host:port]
  * [unix://][username:password@]socketpath
  To specify relative path without `unix://` use `./`.
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
      --config                        to force the centralized config
                                      orchestrator
      --connect-timeout duration      timeout for establishing a binary
                                      connection (default 1s)
      --custom                        to force a custom orchestrator
  -h, --help                          help for bootstrap
  -p, --password string               password for the URI case
      --scope string                  scope the application is looked for in:
                                      project, user or system
      --sslcafile string              path to a trusted certificate authorities
                                      (CA) file for the URI case
      --sslcertfile string            path to an SSL certificate file for the
                                      URI case
      --sslciphers string             colon-separated (:) list of SSL cipher
                                      suites for the URI case
      --sslkeyfile string             path to a private SSL key file for the URI
                                      case
      --sslpassword string            password for decrypting the private SSL
                                      key file for a cluster connection
      --sslpasswordfile string        path to a file containing the private SSL
                                      key password for a cluster connection
      --template string               template of the application the instances
                                      are described in (default "default")
      --timeout int                   timeout (default 10)
  -u, --username string               username for the URI case
      --with-integrity-check string   path to the private key that signs
                                      published data and packages
